All preaching must aim at one of the five objectives of the Church, namely:
• To reveal to humanity the One True God, the Eternal;
• To convert lost souls to the Eternal God;
• To lead men to obey the word of God and to carry out His Law;
• To preserve believers from false doctrines;
• To teach the faithful to live in the fear of the Eternal God in order to merit the Kingdom of Heaven.

The Spiritual Head is entitled to:
• The dignity and respect due to his rank;
• The protection of his person and of his liberties.

The role of the entitled member is:
• To defend the interests of the Association;
• To guarantee the tranquillity and peaceful access of the Association to the land that houses the Spiritual Seat;
• To ensure the continuity of the Association according to the line of conduct laid down by the member by right;
• To protect the benefits attached to the Member by right in accordance with Art. 11 para. 1 of the Statutes;
• To ensure the promotion and defence of the works of the Member by right.
The entitled members have the obligation to train their descendants in the knowledge and practice of the line of conduct defined by the Member by Right.

The role of the member of the clergy is to:
• Assist the Spiritual Head in the accomplishment of his prophetic mission;
• Ensure respect for the doctrine and discipline of the Church;
• Support the adherents morally, spiritually and materially.

The member of the clergy must:
• Be available for any posting;
• Serve the Eternal and in the name of the Eternal, the One true God;
• Respect the doctrine of the Church;
• Respect the code of conduct of his Apostolate as dictated by the Spiritual Head;
• Respect the inviolability of the unity of the Association according to article 106 of the statutes;
Respect the statutes and IR (internal regulations) of the Association:
• Respect and enforce the instructions issued by the Supremacy;
• Support the Church financially, materially and morally;
• Know that he is a sacrifice offered to the Eternal to serve His people with self-denial throughout his life;
This list is not exhaustive.

Once appointed Pontiff, the member of the Clergy must:
• Submit to the orders and to the person of the Spiritual Head;
• Respect the code of conduct of the Pontificate as dictated by the Supremacy;
• Preserve, by irreproachable conduct, the honour and credibility of the Pontificate.
Upon his death, the member of the clergy receives honours and burial ceremonies, and his family may receive occasional assistance from the Church.

The Effective Member must:
• Respect the apolitical character of the Association and refrain from using the Association for political ends;
• Refrain from indulging in debauchery;
• Refrain from leading a life of conflict;
• Refrain from committing acts likely to harm the peace and/or the reputation of the Church or of the country;
• Refrain from committing acts contrary to public morals and public order (forgery and use of forged documents, fraud, brawling, premeditated murder, poisoning, insubordination and disobedience, incitement to revolt or rebellion, rape, embezzlement, falsification of documents, influence peddling);
• Support the Church financially, materially and morally.

The adherent is entitled to:
• Free spiritual assistance;
• Receive doctrinal training through the seminary or through meetings;
• The wearing of the KCC insignia;
• The sacraments conferred by the Church;
• The support of the members of the Church in case of difficulties.
Upon death, the adherent receives honours and burial ceremonies.

The adherent must:
• Attend and support the Church;
• Participate in the sacraments conferred by the Church;
• Comply with Art. 56 of these internal regulations;

According to article 49 of the statutes, the MINPARVIE is responsible for:
• Ensuring the propagation and expansion of KCCISME;
• Ensuring the protection and life of the Church throughout the world;
• Combating the arbitrary use of the consonants KCC and of the effigy of His Eminence the Spiritual Head according to art. 33 of these regulations;
• Guarding against piracy in the printing, reproduction, dissemination and publication of KCCISME according to art. 39 of these regulations;
• Drafting, publishing and collating booklets on the profession of faith, the hymns and any other doctrinal element in order to facilitate the understanding of the members of the Church;
• Ensuring the protection of literary and artistic works and of ensemble hymns according to article 44 of these regulations;
• Producing teaching materials for the initial training of adherents;
Ensuring the continuing training of servants in the doctrine of the Church:
• Ensuring the preservation of sermons, speeches and all electronic media of the Church;
• Ensuring the making, distribution and preservation of priestly vestments;
• Supervising the guidance of the specialized services, in particular: the youth, the AFEBNM, the PGM;
• Periodically taking a census of the members of the association in order to keep track of the membership of the Church
• Proposing to the Supremacy the transfer and accreditation of officers throughout the area where the Church is established;
• initiating the creation of doctrinal training structures on KCCISME;
• initiating the training of all the services of the Church. This list is not exhaustive.

In view of his many and varied responsibilities, the Pontiff in charge of the Minparvie must have:
• A university level of education or equivalent;
• an administrative office composed of a data-entry operator, an archivist, a receptionist, a mail dispatch officer and a statistician;
• specialized departments.

The powers of the Pontiff in charge of the Apostolic Delegation are:
• To ensure regular oversight of the administrative and spiritual activities of the Apostolic Delegates as defined by articles 52 to 54 of the Statutes;
• To oversee the management of the Church by the Apostolic Delegate;
• To transmit to the Apostolic Delegates within his jurisdiction the instructions, guidelines and recommendations of the Supremacy.

In addition to its functioning as defined in articles 40 to 43 of the Statutes, the holding of the sittings of the Assembly of the Clergy is regulated as follows:
• The presence of guests is not authorized;
• At the beginning of its sittings, the assembly completes the composition of its bureau forthwith by electing the 1st and 2nd Rapporteur Secretaries;
• The matters dealt with may be the subject of Minutes to be published throughout the Church.

The Assembly of the Clergy meets every 4 years in Ordinary Session.
It meets in extraordinary session when necessary;
The favourable period for holding the sittings is between the months of July and August.

The lines of material development that are the subject of the General Council are:
• Construction (schools, temples, hospitals, offices, residences for officers, reception centres...);
• The creation of self-financing resources;
• Acquisition (concessions, plots of land, vehicle fleets, etc.);
• The material development programme according to the Church's fields of intervention defined in article 4 of the statutes.

The holding of the sittings of the Assembly of Apostles is regulated as follows:
• The presence of guests is not authorized;
• At the beginning of its sittings, the Assembly of Apostles completes the composition of its bureau by electing the 1st and 2nd Rapporteur Secretaries;
• The pontiffs present within the area where the Mother Church is established are authorized to participate in the sittings of the Assembly of Apostles and have the right to vote.
The favourable period for holding the sittings is between the months of July and August.

The holding of the sittings of the Constituent Assembly is regulated as follows:
• The presence of guests is not authorized;
• At the beginning of its sittings, the Constituent Assembly completes the composition of its bureau by electing the 1st and 2nd Rapporteur Secretaries;
• The draft text is sent to the participants with the invitation so as to enable each member to make his amendments.

The holding of the sittings of the Executive Committee is regulated as follows:
• The timetable drawn up and approved at the beginning of the session must be strictly respected;
• At each session, the president prepares a draft agenda to be submitted to the assessment of the members;
• Before the proceedings begin, the members elect a rapporteur secretary and a deputy rapporteur secretary, the President being designated by the statutes;
• Representation (by proxy) at meetings of the Executive Committee is not authorized;
• The minutes of the session must be read and approved at the end of the sittings;
• Depending on the purpose of the session, the minutes of the session of the Executive Committee may be sent to all parishes.

In his province, the Provincial Apostle represents the Spiritual Head and coordinates the spiritual and material activities of all the parishes;
He oversees the doctrinal and administrative training of the Heads of Parishes at the level of his province.
This list is not exhaustive.

The Provincial Council:
a) Oversees:
• Respect for and application of the doctrine of the Church;
• the development of the entities;
— relations between the heads of the entities, through its president:
• the application of the instructions of the Supremacy and of the Legal Representation;
— the life of the Church in the province:
b) Rules on: - the files submitted to it by the Parish Councils; the cases not falling within the competence of the Parish Council;
c) Decides on the creation of new entities, the appointment of preachers and of the heads of the specialized services, after consulting the Spiritual Head,
d) Examines the files to be forwarded to the Assembly of Apostles.

The cases not falling within the competence of the Provincial Council and for which it must forward the file to the Assembly of Apostles are in particular:
a) The excommunication of a member;
b) The withdrawal of the title of disciples, preachers and Apostles;
c) The rehabilitation of fallen servants;
d) The establishment of an act of divorce;
e) The case of a premeditated abortion;
f) Poisoning or murder;
g) The case of a suspended Apostle;
h) The case of someone who sows division within the Church.

To be promoted to the rank of parish, a section must meet the following criteria:
a) Own a plot of land;
b) Show maturity through the good works of its members;
c) Have a large number of members;
d) Be self-sufficient materially;
e) Have at least three preachers among its servants;
f) The leader must:
• Be a Consecrated Apostle, except in case of shortage, where a preacher may lead the parish;
• reside within the area of the parish;
— have at least post-secondary education.

The Parish Secretary is responsible for:
a) Correspondence;
b) The minutes of the Parish Council and of meetings, if he is a preacher;
c) The management of the archives;
d) Preparing the meetings of the Parish Councils;
e) Making the transfers of funds to the Seat Directorate and to the Provincial Office;
f) Collecting the funds intended for the Parish Committee, in particular the Sunday offerings, etc.;
g) Keeping the parish accounts.

The Worship Leader must:
a) Be an experienced preacher;
b) Be a member of the Parish Council;
c) Be designated by the Parish Council and appointed by the Head of Parish;
d) Have a thorough knowledge of KCCISME and of the regulations governing the service;
e) Enjoy a good testimony from the parishioners and from his household.

The duties of the Worship Leader are to:
a) Draw up the service report;
b) Ensure respect for the liturgy, the discipline and the holiness of the service;
c) Ensure that the preaching conforms to at least one of the objectives of the teaching of the Church;
d) Oversee the whole of the KCCISTE hymnody: songs, rhythm, instrumentation, etc.;
e) Ensure that the servant chosen to preach is kept in the programme of the Sunday service, so that no change is made on this point upon the sudden arrival of another servant, whatever his rank, with the exception of the Spiritual Head and of the Provincial Apostle or of the Legal Representative within his jurisdiction;
f) Refer the matter in writing, with tangible and convincing evidence, to the President of the Provincial Council in the event of flagrant irregularities observed on the part of the President of the Parish Council.

The Parish Council is the spiritual organ of the parish. It is composed of:
a) The Head of Parish, who presides over it;
b) The Deputy Head of Parish;
c) All the consecrated Apostles of the parish;
d) All the Heads of sections;
e) The Parish Secretary, if he is a preacher;
f) The Worship Leader;
g) 2 deaconesses.

The powers of the Parish Council are:
a) To ensure the application of the doctrine in the parish;
b) To rule on the reports of the sections and of the specialized services;
c) To set the programme of preaching of the holy word of the Eternal God;
d) To draw up the calendar for the administration of the sacraments;
e) To examine the promotion of servants;
f) To rule on the discipline of members;
g) To create sections and to appoint and/or relieve their heads;
h) To submit to the Provincial Council the cases not falling within its competence;
i) To designate the coordinators of the specialized services;
j) To ratify the programmes of the sabbath services drawn up by the parish secretary.

In accordance with art. 105 of the Statutes, the Church may collaborate with third parties only in the material field of development, under the following terms:
a) The purpose of the collaboration must be one of the purposes pursued by the Association as defined in art. 4 of the Statutes;
b) The collaborator must refrain from interfering in the doctrine and [discipline] of the Church;
c) The collaborator must respect the apolitical character of the Association;
d) The form, the substance, the execution and other terms of collaboration [are determined] by the Executive Committee with the assistance of the Legal Adviser.
